<b>\", t5, t6, \"<c>\", t9]\nSo final tokens: [\"t0\",\"t1\",\"<b>\",\"t5\",\"t6\",\"<c>\",\"t9\"]? But t6 appears only once? Actually original tokens: t0..t9. After c: t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,<c>,t9. Then b: remove t2,t3,t4, add <b> at index2: t0,t1,<b>,t5,t6,<c>,t9. That's 7 tokens. Is that reasonable? Possibly.\n\nAlternatively, if we select a and c (a score 0.8, c score 0.7), then apply a first (indices 1..3) replace with [\"<a>\"] -> [t0,\"<a>\",t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] (9 tokens? Actually replace two tokens with one: remove t1,t2, insert <a>, so length becomes 9). Then apply c (indices 7..9) on original indices? But after a, the indices shifted. That's messy. So better to apply in reverse order of start index.\n\nWithout clear spec, we need to guess the intended algorithm. The task name \"AGENT-TF-02\" and the registry_id \"A\" suggest this is a standard test. Possibly the algorithm is to select the call with the highest score, then remove overlapping calls, repeat. That is a typical greedy interval selection. Then produce final tokens by applying selected calls in reverse order.\n\nWe need to output a solve function. The evaluator will run against hidden test cases. So we must adhere to some expected logic. Since we don't have expected outputs, we must design a sensible algorithm. I'll assume: \n- Sort calls by score descending.\n- Iterate, select call if it doesn't overlap with any already selected (consider intervals [start, end) inclusive start exclusive end).\n- Then apply selected calls in order of increasing start index (or decreasing to avoid index shift) to produce final tokens: for each selected call, replace tokens from start to end (exclusive) with its replacement_tokens.\n- Then output selected_call_ids (in the order they were selected? or original order? Probably original order of appearance in calls array? But the output schema just says array of strings, order might not matter, but likely we should preserve order as they appear in input? The fixture doesn't specify. I'll preserve the order from the input calls array, only including selected ones.\n\nLet's test with the given fixture. Using greedy by score: b (0.9) selected, a (0.8) overlaps with b, so skipped, c (0.7) selected. So selected_call_ids = [\"b\",\"c\"] (if we preserve input order: a,b,c -> filtered: b,c in order of input: b then c? Actually input order: a,b,c. Filtered: b is at index1, c at index2 -> order [b,c]. That's fine). Then apply replacements: sort by start ascending: b start=2, c start=7. To avoid index shift, apply in descending order: c first (start=7, end=9), then b (start=2, end=5). Result tokens as computed: [t0,t1,\"<b>\",t5,t6,\"<c>\",t9]? Did we lose t7 and t8? Actually original t7 and t8 are within c's span (7..9 exclusive: indices 7,8) are replaced by \"<c>\". So after c, t7 and t8 removed, \"<c>\" inserted. Then b replaces t2,t3,t4 with \"<b>\". So final: indices: 0:t0,1:t1,2:\"<b>\",3:t5,4:t6,5:\"<c>\",6:t9. That's 7 tokens. Wait we have t9 at index6. That seems plausible.\n\nAlternatively, if we select a and c: a (0.8) and c (0.7) - no overlap between a and c. Then selected_call_ids = [\"a\",\"c\"]. Apply: sort by start: a=1,c=7. Apply in reverse: c first then a. After c: [t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,\"<c>\",t9] (9 tokens). Then a: replace indices1..3 (original indices) but after c, indices have shifted? Since a start=1 is before c, applying c first doesn't affect indices <7, so indices 1..3 are still t1,t2,t3. Replace with [\"<a>\"]: [t0,\"<a>\",t4,t5,t6,\"<c>\",t9] (7 tokens). That's also 7 tokens. Which one is more likely? The greedy by score selects b and c, giving a different result. Without expected output, it's ambiguous.\n\nMaybe the algorithm is to select calls that maximize total score or something. But given we have no training data, we must assume a simple deterministic algorithm. The problem statement says \"registered procedure task AGENT-TF-02\", implying there is a known procedure. Since I'm an AI, I might have been trained on standard tasks. Possibly it's \"Token Fusion\" where we merge overlapping calls by selecting the highest score and then fusing.\n\nAnother clue: The task name \"AGENT-TF-02\" and the presence of \"replacement_tokens\" suggests that the calls represent function calls that replace tokens. The algorithm might be to simulate the application of all calls (like in order of some priority) but handle overlaps. Could be similar to \"agent token fusion\" where we apply all calls that are non-conflicting after resolution.\n\nGiven the lack of expected output, I'll go with the greedy selection by score and non-overlap, and then apply replacements in reverse start order.\n\nI will implement `solve(case)` accordingly.\n\nNote: The input may have \"calls\" field, and \"tokens\" field. We must output \"selected_call_ids\" and \"tokens\". The output tokens array is the result after applying replacements.\n\nEdge cases: If no calls selected, output empty list and original tokens. If calls have overlapping but same score, need a tie-breaker. I'll use original order.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Get tokens list and calls list from case.\n2. Sort calls by score descending, then maybe by start index ascending as tie-breaker.\n3. selected = [] list of call objects.\n4. selected_intervals = [] list of (start, end) as ints.\n5. For each call in sorted calls:\n   - Check if interval [start, end) overlaps with any interval in selected_intervals.\n   - Overlap condition: not (call.end <= other.start or call.start >= other.end). Since intervals are [start, end).\n   - If no overlap, add to selected, and add its interval.\n6. Sort selected calls by start ascending (or descending for replacement). For replacement, we will iterate selected calls sorted by start descending to avoid index shifts.\n7. Build new_tokens = list(case['tokens'])\n8. For call in reversed(sorted selected by start asc): Actually we need to apply in descending order of start. So sort by start descending.\n   - start,